+## bin/clean-workspaces.sh
+
+Removes stale build workspaces. A workspace is a directory directly under the root; it is
+stale when its newest file is more than `DAYS` days old (an empty directory is aged by its own
+modification time). Symlinks under the root are skipped, and the filesystem root `/` is refused.
+
+```
+bin/clean-workspaces.sh [-n] [-r ROOT] [-d DAYS]
+```
+
+| Option | Default | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|
+| `-r`, `--root ROOT` | `workspaces` | Directory that holds the workspaces (relative paths resolve from the current directory) |
+| `-d`, `--days DAYS` | `7` | Age threshold in days; non-negative integer |
+| `-n`, `--dry-run` | off | List what would be removed (`would remove NAME (age Nd)`) and remove nothing |
+| `-h`, `--help` | | Show usage |
+
+Examples:
+
+```
+bin/clean-workspaces.sh                                  # workspaces/, 7 days
+bin/clean-workspaces.sh --dry-run -r /var/ci/work -d 3   # preview only
+bin/clean-workspaces.sh -r /var/ci/work -d 3
+```
+
+Exit status: `0` on success, `1` if a workspace could not be aged or removed, `64` for a usage
+error, `66` if the root is not a directory.
